<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Hanning Speke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1827">1827</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Hanning Speke was a British army officer and explorer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. On Richard Burton's expedition he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> in 1858 and identified it as the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, a claim he set out to prove with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-augustus-grant">James Augustus Grant</a> in 1860–63, travelling through Karagwe and Buganda to the lake's outlet. He died in a shooting accident in 1864, on the eve of a public debate with Burton.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke.jpg" alt="John Hanning Speke" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Southwell Brothers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JH_Speke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
